Wall Street ended another tumultuous  week with another big loss on Friday the  Dow fell more than 900 points but the  week also saw the biggest one-day point  gain in history following word that a  two trillion dollar economic relief  package would pass Congress the  coronavirus aid relief and Economic  Security Act or cares was signed into  law by President Trump on Friday joining  us to talk about what is in the package  and how it may affect you is CBS news  senior business analyst Jill Schlesinger  Jill let's start right there how is this  bill going to affect Americans what are  the big takeaways I think the big  takeaways is that that we really are  seeing a lifeline come from Washington  DC we've got three aspects big aspects  of the plan and we think about  individuals first well you mentioned  earlier in the show those checks $1,200  checks to anyone who makes less than  $75,000 that amount does faze lower as  you approach $99,000 just know that if  you make less than 99 thousand dollars  as a household your adjusted gross  income you're going to get a check  there's also another $500 per child very  helpful again have to be part of that  income but for individuals the biggest  aspect to me is the expanding of the  unemployment benefits so the government  is tacking on 13 weeks to whatever the  maximum is in your state and also  providing a check of $600 a week on top  of whatever your state unemployment  benefit is so that's for the individuals  there's relief for small businesses and  corporations I think the small business  part is really important there are loans  that are available to cover your payroll  your rent your utilities and those loans  from the SBA through the Small Business  Administration would be forgiven as long  as you have the same amount of money  going out towards payroll that you for  the next eight weeks that you did in the  prior run up to the virus so when do  people get these checks and how well you  know it's going to be really interesting  from the checks the direct payments if  you have a record on file with the  government of a direct deposit so maybe  you're a Social Security recipient  maybe you filed your taxes  electronically that's going to be the  way you get your money it'll be a few  weeks but if for some reason you still  get paper checks this could take up to  two three some people who take even four  months simply because the government  doesn't have the facility to print  checks and this couldn't hold you up  unemployment benefits should be coming  pretty quickly after you apply state  systems got clogged up but we're hearing  two to three weeks is when the  unemployment benefits should be received  so we've talked about this economy  feeling like it just came to a  standstill as we're starting to feel  some of the impact can you look ahead a  few months to have any indication as to  how much worse it may get well I think  that we're at the tip here and we're  clearly in a recession we had the IMF  chief say that the world is already in a  recession many of the economists I speak  to say that this is at least sort of a  march through the summer event so we're  going to see a recession which is  loosely defined as two negative quarters  in a row but I think that we're going to  see the economy really come to a halt  right now slowly start to begin to  unwind a little bit and then hopefully  by the time at the end of the summer  into the end of the year we should come  back and I put an asterisk on that that  is predicated say economists on the fact  that the virus is contained they worried  that if we reopen too quickly we dip  down again and that's why they're  watching very closely what a lot of  these governors are doing that's the big  question Jill about whether this is a  v-shaped recovery or a W and that is  whether you sink down and come right  back up or or dip along a little bit and  continue to have trouble right I think  that the the first part of this when we  first were hearing about it everybody  did talk about that nice big V then we  started in the middle of it and we say  oh gosh maybe it's an L like you just go  down and muddle along the bottom but I  think that we're sort of looking at  probably a you and hopefully that means  that we sort of bounce around the bottom  and then come back up the W which was a  double-dip recession meaning we seem to  be better things open up again but  the virus continues to spread and then  people freaked out that's what we would  like to avoid one big plug here if  you're a small business go to sba.gov  there's great information on there how  you can access those loan programs  always a good idea to support small  business Jill thank you very much
 

